AI agents, also called agentic AI, are autonomous software systems that pursue goals, reason with large language models (LLMs), and interact with their environment using a combination of memory, planning, tool interfaces, and orchestration software. Unlike narrow, task‑specific tools such as 2023 chatbots, an AI agent can perceive input, decide on a next step, invoke and evaluate external services, and adjust its plan until a task is finished—often employing a continuous sense‑plan‑act‑reflect cycle that lets it learn from past interactions. Common features include goal‑directed behavior, the ability to use and control third‑party tools, and the capacity to perform multi‑step tasks autonomously. Practical uses span from booking travel to automating complex business workflows; vendors and consulting firms highlight the productivity gains, consistency, and scalability these agents bring to enterprises, while guidance from OpenAI, IBM, AWS, and other tech leaders stresses that the core of an agent is an LLM coupled with a framework that gives the agent “hands” to act and “eyes” to observe. In short, AI agents represent the next evolution of generative AI, moving from a tool‑like assistant to a self‑directed, reasoning agent that can plan, collaborate, and improve its performance over time.